WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.046
Can you stop someone completing a line, without trying to complete one yourself?

2
00:00:05.046 --> 00:00:08.642
That is the Maker-Breaker version of noughts and crosses.

3
00:00:08.642 --> 00:00:11.228
Maker moves first and wants a whole line.

4
00:00:11.228 --> 00:00:14.256
Breaker only needs to spoil every possible line.

5
00:00:14.633 --> 00:00:19.717
Here is a five by five board. Each repeated number marks a pair of cells.

6
00:00:19.717 --> 00:00:24.940
Every row, every column, and both long diagonals contain one complete pair.

7
00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:30.233
The centre is unpaired. Watch what happens when Maker takes a numbered cell.

8
00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:36.296
Breaker takes its partner. Whatever Maker tries next, answer in the paired cell.

9
00:00:36.296 --> 00:00:39.215
Maker can never own both cells of a pair.

10
00:00:39.215 --> 00:00:45.267
Since every winning line contains a pair, every line will contain a cell Maker cannot

11
00:00:45.267 --> 00:00:45.552
own.

12
00:00:45.933 --> 00:00:48.649
Notice why the pairs must not overlap.

13
00:00:48.649 --> 00:00:53.007
One cell cannot serve as the answer to two different threats.

14
00:00:53.007 --> 00:00:56.365
We want each line to receive two private cells.

15
00:00:56.365 --> 00:01:01.581
This turns a game strategy into a problem about assigning distinct cells.

16
00:01:01.967 --> 00:01:05.010
Now stack seven square boards into a cube.

17
00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:11.388
A winning line can run along an axis, across a flat slice diagonally, or from one corner

18
00:01:11.388 --> 00:01:16.823
to its opposite corner. On the seven-cube, every such line has seven cells.

19
00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:22.821
There are one hundred and forty seven axis lines, forty two plane diagonals, and four

20
00:01:22.821 --> 00:01:26.987
space diagonals. That makes one hundred and ninety three lines.

21
00:01:26.987 --> 00:01:32.608
Giving every line two private cells would require three hundred and eighty six cells.

22
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:37.230
But the cube has only three hundred and forty three cells.

23
00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:40.585
So a pairing cannot work from the empty board.

24
00:01:40.585 --> 00:01:45.180
Breaker must first claim cells that kill several lines at once.

25
00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:49.848
A killed line already contains a Breaker mark and needs no pair.

26
00:01:50.233 --> 00:01:54.686
To find a pairing later, draw two slots for every surviving line.

27
00:01:54.686 --> 00:01:57.974
Connect each slot to the free cells on its line.

28
00:01:57.974 --> 00:02:01.605
Our task is to assign a different cell to every slot.

29
00:02:01.605 --> 00:02:04.345
Such an assignment is called a matching.

30
00:02:04.733 --> 00:02:09.623
A local shortage can ruin everything. These three slots can reach only two cells.

31
00:02:09.623 --> 00:02:14.151
Even if many other cells exist elsewhere, one of these slots must lose out.

32
00:02:14.151 --> 00:02:18.437
The shortage belongs to this group, not necessarily to the whole board.

33
00:02:18.833 --> 00:02:22.647
Hall's theorem says that this is the only kind of obstruction.

34
00:02:22.647 --> 00:02:27.813
A matching exists exactly when every group of slots can reach at least as many cells

35
00:02:27.813 --> 00:02:33.041
as the group contains. Every group matters, including ones that overlap other groups.

36
00:02:33.433 --> 00:02:38.750
Now ask for spare capacity. Suppose a group of four slots can reach seven cells.

37
00:02:38.750 --> 00:02:44.599
Its surplus is three. But the surplus of the entire graph is the smallest spare capacity

38
00:02:44.599 --> 00:02:48.985
over every nonempty group of slots, not just this visible example.

39
00:02:49.367 --> 00:02:53.908
If every group has at least three spare cells, delete any three cells.

40
00:02:53.908 --> 00:02:59.227
Each group loses at most three of its neighbours, so every group still has enough.

41
00:02:59.227 --> 00:03:04.223
Hall's condition survives. The new matching may differ from the old matching.

42
00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:09.516
That is the key move. Unknown Maker choices simply delete available cells.

43
00:03:09.516 --> 00:03:15.230
If the graph has enough surplus, we do not need to enumerate where those choices went.

44
00:03:15.230 --> 00:03:18.352
A single robust certificate covers all of them.

45
00:03:18.733 --> 00:03:22.301
How do we measure the minimum without listing every group?

46
00:03:22.301 --> 00:03:26.238
Temporarily add copies of one slot and ask for a matching again.

47
00:03:26.238 --> 00:03:31.713
If every slot can tolerate three extra copies, every group must have at least three spare

48
00:03:31.713 --> 00:03:32.389
neighbours.

49
00:03:32.767 --> 00:03:35.619
Why? Pick any group, then pick a slot inside it.

50
00:03:35.619 --> 00:03:39.778
That group plus its three copies now needs three extra distinct cells.

51
00:03:39.778 --> 00:03:44.828
Passing the test supplies them. Because a line's two slots have identical neighbours,

52
00:03:44.828 --> 00:03:47.383
only one test per surviving line is needed.

53
00:03:47.767 --> 00:03:48.539
Consider Maker opening at the centre of the seven-cube.

54
00:03:48.539 --> 00:03:49.747
Breaker first takes five, five, five. Over the next three rounds, Breaker takes a free

55
00:03:49.747 --> 00:03:50.983
cell on each of the other space diagonals, favouring cells that kill the most new lines.

56
00:03:55.167 --> 00:03:58.702
Four Breaker cells can therefore block many lines.

57
00:03:58.702 --> 00:04:04.712
In the successful opening they meet twenty seven lines, leaving one hundred and sixty

58
00:04:04.712 --> 00:04:10.651
six alive. The certificate temporarily remembers only Maker's centre cell and leaves

59
00:04:10.651 --> 00:04:13.479
the other three Maker moves unspecified.

60
00:04:13.867 --> 00:04:19.167
Every checked central configuration meeting twenty six or twenty seven lines has surplus

61
00:04:19.167 --> 00:04:24.226
at least four. Removing the three unknown Maker cells therefore preserves a pairing.

62
00:04:24.226 --> 00:04:29.587
At that point Breaker switches to the partner-response strategy for the rest of the game.

63
00:04:29.967 --> 00:04:33.827
The opening rule must also remain legal against every reply.

64
00:04:33.827 --> 00:04:39.617
A separate tree checks the diagonal-relevant choices: ten thousand, six hundred and twenty

65
00:04:39.617 --> 00:04:43.027
seven leaves, leading to fourteen final Breaker sets.

66
00:04:43.027 --> 00:04:45.279
Every one meets twenty seven lines.

67
00:04:45.667 --> 00:04:50.963
The full central check includes one thousand, two hundred and ninety six possible sets.

68
00:04:50.963 --> 00:04:56.199
Five hundred and seventy six meet twenty seven lines, and two hundred and eighty eight

69
00:04:56.199 --> 00:05:01.435
meet twenty six. Both groups have surplus at least four, not necessarily exactly four.

70
00:05:01.833 --> 00:05:04.762
The remaining groups show why placement matters.

71
00:05:04.762 --> 00:05:09.581
Three hundred and eighty four sets meet twenty five lines and have surplus two.

72
00:05:09.581 --> 00:05:13.181
Forty eight meet only twenty two lines and have no pairing.

73
00:05:13.181 --> 00:05:16.353
The opening rule avoids these weaker configurations.

74
00:05:16.733 --> 00:05:21.233
If Maker starts elsewhere, Breaker takes the centre instead.

75
00:05:21.233 --> 00:05:27.533
Next choose a free cell on a space diagonal, then a cell on another diagonal sharing

76
00:05:27.533 --> 00:05:34.058
no line with it. Each first choice has twelve eligible partners, so Maker cannot occupy

77
00:05:34.058 --> 00:05:34.733
them all.

78
00:05:35.133 --> 00:05:38.818
These three Breaker cells meet twenty five lines.

79
00:05:38.818 --> 00:05:43.781
The checker remembers Maker's first move and ignores the next two.

80
00:05:43.781 --> 00:05:50.473
Across all forty eight thousand, nine hundred and sixty legal configurations, the surplus

81
00:05:50.473 --> 00:05:56.037
is exactly three. Two unknown moves fit comfortably inside that allowance.

82
00:05:56.433 --> 00:06:02.019
An independent reconstruction checks those configurations in two equal shards, each with

83
00:06:02.019 --> 00:06:05.320
twenty four thousand, four hundred and eighty cases.

84
00:06:05.320 --> 00:06:10.588
A symmetry-reduced run agrees on one thousand, one hundred and fifty eight classes.

85
00:06:10.588 --> 00:06:14.841
These are positions checked, each requiring several matching tests.

86
00:06:15.233 --> 00:06:20.859
The seven-cube result was already proved by Cain, Raymond, and Kallersjo in twenty twenty

87
00:06:20.859 --> 00:06:26.169
five. This work supplies a shorter certificate and repairs a gap in the written case

88
00:06:26.169 --> 00:06:30.593
analysis. It does not overturn the known conclusion that Breaker wins.

89
00:06:30.967 --> 00:06:37.383
The old non-central argument assumed both of Maker's first two cells could be moved into

90
00:06:37.383 --> 00:06:42.924
the same half-cube by symmetry. But take one, seven, one, and six, two, six.

91
00:06:42.924 --> 00:06:48.319
Relative to the centre, their signs are opposite in all three coordinates.

92
00:06:48.700 --> 00:06:52.276
Permuting axes or reversing them preserves that opposition.

93
00:06:52.276 --> 00:06:56.457
Along whichever axis becomes horizontal, one cell stays on each side.

94
00:06:56.457 --> 00:06:59.124
They cannot both enter the stated half-cube.

95
00:06:59.124 --> 00:07:04.215
There are two thousand, nine hundred and sixteen unordered pairs like this, or twice

96
00:07:04.215 --> 00:07:05.548
as many ordered pairs.

97
00:07:05.933 --> 00:07:11.387
For the concrete published move rule, Maker can occupy both proposed third replies.

98
00:07:11.387 --> 00:07:14.279
The new flexible opening covers these cases.

99
00:07:14.279 --> 00:07:19.996
The authors' non-central code was not available in the inspected public package, so the

100
00:07:19.996 --> 00:07:23.741
criticism concerns the written analysis, not unseen code.

101
00:07:24.133 --> 00:07:26.660
Why not solve the six-cube in the same way?

102
00:07:26.660 --> 00:07:31.067
It has two hundred and sixteen cells and one hundred and forty eight lines.

103
00:07:31.067 --> 00:07:35.298
After seven complete rounds, only two hundred and two cells remain free.

104
00:07:35.298 --> 00:07:38.941
At most one hundred and one lines could receive private pairs.

105
00:07:39.333 --> 00:07:43.427
So Breaker would need to have killed at least forty seven lines.

106
00:07:43.427 --> 00:07:49.056
But seven Breaker cells can meet at most forty six: at most six non-space-diagonal lines

107
00:07:49.056 --> 00:07:52.382
per cell, plus only four space diagonals altogether.

108
00:07:52.382 --> 00:07:53.853
Forty six is one short.

109
00:07:54.233 --> 00:07:57.406
This excludes a pairing before Breaker's eighth move.

110
00:07:57.406 --> 00:08:00.039
It does not exclude every possible strategy.

111
00:08:00.039 --> 00:08:05.067
There is another limit: one surviving six-cell line has two slots, so its surplus is

112
00:08:05.067 --> 00:08:08.897
at most four, and smaller if Maker already occupies cells on it.

113
00:08:09.267 --> 00:08:14.892
The six by six by six game remains open, as does five by five by five in the accepted

114
00:08:14.892 --> 00:08:20.649
research scope. The seven-cube proof succeeds because enough lines can be killed early,

115
00:08:20.649 --> 00:08:25.083
and the remaining matching tolerates all the unknown opening moves.

116
00:08:25.467 --> 00:08:30.806
Lean checks the abstract Hall replication, surplus, deletion-matching argument, and counting

117
00:08:30.806 --> 00:08:35.913
obstruction. It does not check the actual board enumeration or formalize the whole game.

118
00:08:35.913 --> 00:08:40.904
The finite board certificates come from exact programs; the game strategy is justified

119
00:08:40.904 --> 00:08:42.123
in the written proof.

120
00:08:42.500 --> 00:08:47.969
Remember three ideas: pair cells to block lines, test every group for spare capacity,

121
00:08:47.969 --> 00:08:51.315
and use that spare capacity to absorb unknown moves.

122
00:08:51.315 --> 00:08:55.883
Correctness confidence is high for this checked seven-cube certificate.

123
00:08:55.883 --> 00:09:01.481
Originality of the surplus application remains moderately confident, within the sources

124
00:09:01.481 --> 00:09:02.060
reviewed.
